1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
People and businesses do a lot of stuff.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:11,340
How do you take this useful stuff that we do and break it down into discrete steps for the agentic workflow to follow?

3
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,620
Let's take a look.

4
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,680
Take the example of building a research agent.

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:23,940
If you want an AI system to write an essay on a topic X, one thing you could do is prompt an ELN to have it generate an output directly.

6
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:28,420
But if you were to do this for topics that you want deeply researched,

7
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:34,520
you may find that the ELN output covers only the surface level points or maybe covers only the obvious facts,

8
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:38,480
but doesn't go as deep into the subject as you want it to.

9
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:45,000
In this case, you might then reflect on how you as a human would write an essay on a certain topic.

10
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,800
Would you just sit down and start writing?

11
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:51,800
Or would you take multiple steps such as first write an essay outline and then search the rep

12
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:55,040
and then based on the input from the web search, write the essay.

13
00:00:55,040 --> 00:01:00,900
And as I take a task and decompose it into steps, one question I'm always asking myself is,

14
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:11,040
if I look at these steps 1, 2, and 3, can each of them be done either by an ELN or by a short piece of code or by a function call or by a tool?

15
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:19,440
And so in this case, I think that an ELN can maybe write a decent outline on many topics that I would want it to help me think through.

16
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:27,300
So I'd say probably OK on the first step. And then I know how to use an ELN to generate search terms to search the web.

17
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:34,440
So I would say the second step is also doable. And then based on web search, I think an ELN could input the web search results and write an essay.

18
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:42,400
And so this would be a reasonable first attempt at an agentic workflow for writing an essay that goes deeper than just direct generation.

19
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:49,640
But if I were to then implement this agentic workflow and look at the results, maybe you find that the results still aren't good enough.

20
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:54,380
It's still not yet as deeply thoughtful or maybe the essays feel a little bit disjointed.

21
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:58,140
This has actually happened to me once built a research agent using this as a workflow.

22
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:04,380
But when I read the output, it felt a bit disjointed. You know, the start of the article didn't feel completely consistent with the middle,

23
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:11,260
didn't feel completely consistent with the end. In this case, what you might do is then reflect on how you would change the workflow.

24
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:14,600
if you as a human found that the essay is a little bit disjointed.

25
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:22,540
One thing you could do is take the third step and further decompose, write the essay into additional steps.

26
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:28,200
So instead of writing the essay all in one go, you might instead have it write the first draft,

27
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:32,120
and then consider what parts need revision, and then revise the draft.

28
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:39,400
And this would be how I as a human might go about it to not just write the final essay at my first attempt,

29
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:45,560
but read the first draft and then read over it, which is another step that the OM is pretty decent at.

30
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:49,100
And then based on my own critique of my own essay, I'll revise the draft.

31
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:55,900
So the recap, I started off with direct generation, just one step, decided that it wasn't good enough,

32
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:00,100
and so broke that down into three steps, and then maybe decided that it still isn't good enough,

33
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:05,640
and took one of the steps and further broken it down or decomposed it into three more steps,

34
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:11,060
resulting in this more complex, richer process for generating an essay.

35
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:15,260
And depending on how satisfied you are with the results of this process,

36
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:19,560
you may choose to even modify this essay generation process further.

37
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:25,640
Let's look at the second example of how to decompose complex tasks into smaller steps.

38
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:30,060
Take the example of responding to basic customer order inquiries.

39
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:34,280
The first step that the human customer service agent might carry out might be to first

40
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:38,960
extract the key information, such as who is this email from, what did they order,

41
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:43,200
and what is the order number. And these are things that an OM could do,

42
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:48,020
so I could just say let's have an OM do that. The second step would be to then find the relevant

43
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:54,160
customer records, so to write and generate the relevant database queries to pull up the order of

44
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:59,060
what the customer had ordered and what I shipped and so on. I think an OM, with the ability to call

45
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:06,040
a function to query the orders database, should really do that. And lastly, having pulled up

46
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:10,940
the customer record or the customer order record, I might then write and send a response back to

47
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:15,920
the customer. And I think with the information we pulled up, this third step is also doable with an OM,

48
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:22,060
if I give it the option to call an API to send an email. So this would be another example of taking a

49
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:28,040
task of responding to customer email and breaking it down into three individual steps, where I can look at

50
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:33,940
each of these steps and say, yep, I think an OM or an OM with the ability to call a function to query

51
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:39,660
a database or send an email should be able to do that. Just one last example for the invoice processing.

52
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:45,760
After a PDF invoice has been converted to text, the first step is to pull out the required information,

53
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:50,920
the name of the biller, the address, the due date, the amount due, and so on. And now should be able to

54
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:56,740
do that. And then if I want to check that the information was extracted and save it in a new

55
00:04:56,740 --> 00:05:02,340
database entry, then I think an OM should be able to help me call a function to update the database

56
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:08,320
record. And so to implement this, we'll implement an agentic workflow to carry out basically these two

57
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:14,160
steps. When building agentic workflows, I think of myself as having a number of building blocks.

58
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:19,300
One important building block would be large language models or maybe large multimodal models,

59
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:24,280
if I want to try to process images or audio as well. And OMs are good at generating text,

60
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:29,780
deciding what to call, maybe extracting information. For some highly specialized tasks,

61
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:36,300
I might also use some other AI models, such as an AI model for converting a PDF to text,

62
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:42,680
or for text-to-speech, or for image analysis. In addition to AI models, I also have access to

63
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:48,480
a number of software tools, including different APIs that I can call to do real-time search,

64
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:53,740
to get maybe real-time weather data, to send emails, check calendar, and so on.

65
00:05:53,740 --> 00:06:01,040
And I might also have tools to retrieve information, to pull up data from a database, or to invent

66
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:06,000
RAG, or retrieval augmented generation, where I can look up a large text database and find the most

67
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,800
relevant text. Or I might also have tools to execute code. And this is a tool that lets an alum

68
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:17,940
write code and then run the code on your computer to do a huge range of things. In case some of these

69
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:22,700
tools seem a bit foreign to you, don't worry about it. We'll go through the most important tools in much

70
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:27,940
greater detail in a later module. But I think of a lot of my work when I'm building an agentic

71
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:32,700
workflow as looking at the work that the person or business is doing, and then trying to figure

72
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:37,880
out with these building blocks, how can I sequence these building blocks together in order to carry

73
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:43,800
out the tasks that I want my system to carry out. And this is why having a good understanding of what

74
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:49,640
building blocks are available, which I hope you have a better sense of by the end of this course as well,

75
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:54,220
will allow you to better envision what agentic workflows you can build by combining these

76
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:59,300
building blocks together. So to summarize, one of the key skills in building agentic workflows

77
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:06,440
is to look at a bunch of stuff that maybe someone does and to identify the discrete steps that it

78
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:11,740
could be implemented with. And when I'm looking at the individual discrete steps, one question I'm

79
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:18,640
always asking myself is can this step be implemented with either an LM or with one of the tools such as

80
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:25,020
an API or a function call that I have access to? And in case the answer is no, I'll then often ask myself

81
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:30,640
how would I as a human do this step and is it possible to decompose this further or break this

82
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:37,840
down into even smaller steps that then maybe is more amenable to implementation with an LM or with

83
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:43,680
one of the software tools that I have. So I hope this gives you a rough sense of how to think about

84
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:48,840
task decomposition. In case you feel like you don't fully have it yet, don't worry about it.

85
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:53,220
We'll go through many more examples in this course and you have a much better understanding of this

86
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:59,040
by the end of this course. But it turns out that as you build agentic workflows, you find that often

87
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:05,400
you build an initial task decomposition, initial agentic workflow, and then you want to keep on

88
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:10,920
iterating and improving on it quite a few times until it delivers the level of performance that you

89
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:16,800
want. And to drive this improvement process, which I found important for many projects, one of the key

90
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:23,280
skills is to know how to evaluate your agentic workflow. So in the next video, we'll talk about

91
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:29,960
evaluations or evals and just be a key component of how you can build and then also keep on improving

92
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:34,860
your workflows to get the performance that you want. Let's talk about evals in the next video.

